I stopped issue #3 without committing or tagging anything. It needs a decision only an org admin can make, so I commented on the issue and added the `needs-decision` label.

**The blocker:** Release CI has to build the image from the tag, and the build first has to run `tacplus build`. When I ran it on main (tacplus 2.4.1), it cloned six private repos: `registry`, `eventbus`, `letsencrypt`, `kernel`, `stratum` and `opa`. In GitHub Actions the default `GITHUB_TOKEN` can only read stratum itself, and this repo has no other secret set. llmproxy's release works without one only because it has no private dependencies. mailbox#2, messaging#2 and gateway#6 are already waiting on this same question.

**What I asked for:** one org secret (I suggested the name `TACPLUS_REGISTRY_TOKEN`). It would be a read-only token on the registry and the service repos that stratum generates from; because of `records: all`, that is in practice every service repo with a record. I asked what the secret should be called and whether this repo will be able to see it. The compiler fix that would let it use a token from the environment, tacplus#191, is also still open, so the workflow may need a git credential helper for now.

**What I'll do once it exists:** follow llmproxy's working release flow, which my comment lists in full:
- the gates in CI, plus a `just gates` recipe (the justfile has none);
- the release workflow;
- the scripts that pick the image tags and write the notes, with tests;
- a "Releasing" section in the README;
- then tag `v0.1.0` and dry-run `v0.1.1`.

The build itself isn't the problem: none of stratum's Maven dependencies come from SeaBeam's GitLab package repository, which the pom still lists.

I saved a checkpoint to `looper/stratum-3` saying what is done and what is left. It reported 2 files changed although the checkout had no changes, which I didn't look into. The issue is still open.
